  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1) \nThe airmass across our area has destabilized nicely this afternoon\nwith MLCapes currently in the 2000-2500 J/KG range and this will\nallow for isolated showers and thunderstorms to develop early this\nafternoon. There is a weak low level convergent boundary/trough axis\nthat will shift southeast into our area later this afternoon into \nthis evening and this may serve as a focus for somewhat better \ndevelopment as we head through the rest of the day. While coverage \nshould not be as widespread as what we saw on Friday, it will still \nbe tough to rule out a few strong to severe thunderstorms. Damaging \nwind will be the main threat, along with the possibility for locally\nheavy downpours. \n\nIn west to northwest flow aloft, additional mid level short waves \nwill drop down across the southern Great Lakes and into the upper\nOhio Valley over the next several days. There is some uncertainty \nfor Sunday into Sunday night with how well a potential MCS off to \nour northwest will hold together as it moves down toward our area. \nFor now, will limit pops to chance or less, mainly across northern \nportions of our area. Beyond that, timing and placement remains problematic,\nbut occasional rounds of showers and storms can be expected Monday \nthrough at least Wednesday, but possibly lingering through the end \nof the week, as we remain in northwest flow aloft. \n\nDeeper layer shear will trend upward through mid week, so the threat\nfor severe storms will likely continue through at least Wednesday. \nLocally heavy rainfall will also remain possible with isolated \nflooding a continued threat. More widespread flood concerns may \nalso develop if we start to get repeated rounds of heavy rainfall \nover the same locations.",
